Source: Cpp Core Guidelines > <a name="s-functions"></a>F: Functions > <a name="ss-fct-def"></a>F.def: Function definitions > <a name="rf-constexpr"></a>F.4: If a function might have to be evaluated at compile time, declare it `constexpr` > Reason > Example

##### Example

The (in)famous factorial:

 constexpr int fac(int n)
 {
 constexpr int max_exp = 17; // constexpr enables max_exp to be used in Expects
 Expects(0 <= n && n < max_exp); // prevent silliness and overflow
 int x = 1;
 for (int i = 2; i <= n; ++i) x *= i;
 return x;
 }

This is C++14.
For C++11, use a recursive formulation of `fac()`.

