Source: Cpp Core Guidelines > <a name="s-functions"></a>F: Functions > <a name="ss-call"></a>F.call: Parameter passing > <a name="rf-default-args"></a>F.51: Where there is a choice, prefer default arguments over overloading > Reason > Note

##### Note

There is a choice between using default argument and overloading when the alternatives are from a set of arguments of the same types.
For example:

 void print(const string& s, format f = {});

as opposed to

 void print(const string& s); // use default format
 void print(const string& s, format f);

There is not a choice when a set of functions are used to do a semantically equivalent operation to a set of types. For example:

 void print(const char&);
 void print(int);
 void print(zstring);

