Source: Cpp Core Guidelines > <a name="s-class"></a>C: Classes and class hierarchies > <a name="ss-dtor"></a>C.dtor: Destructors > <a name="rc-dtor"></a>C.30: Define a destructor if a class needs an explicit action at object destruction > Reason > Example

##### Example

 template<typename A>
 struct final_action { // slightly simplified
 A act;
 final_action(A a) : act{a} {}
 ~final_action() { act(); }
 };

 template<typename A>
 final_action<A> finally(A act) // deduce action type
 {
 return final_action<A>{act};
 }

 void test()
 {
 auto act = finally([] { cout << "Exit test\n"; }); // establish exit action
 // ...
 if (something) return; // act done here
 // ...
 } // act done here

The whole purpose of `final_action` is to get a piece of code (usually a lambda) executed upon destruction.

