Source: Cpp Core Guidelines > <a name="s-class"></a>C: Classes and class hierarchies > C.other: Other default operation rules > <a name="rc-eqdefault"></a>C.80: Use `=default` if you have to be explicit about using the default semantics > Reason > Example

##### Example

 class Tracer {
 string message;
 public:
 Tracer(const string& m) : message{m} { cerr << "entering " << message << '\n'; }
 ~Tracer() { cerr << "exiting " << message << '\n'; }

 Tracer(const Tracer&) = default;
 Tracer& operator=(const Tracer&) = default;
 Tracer(Tracer&&) noexcept = default;
 Tracer& operator=(Tracer&&) noexcept = default;
 };

Because we defined the destructor, we must define the copy and move operations. The `= default` is the best and simplest way of doing that.

