Source: Cpp Core Guidelines > <a name="s-class"></a>C: Classes and class hierarchies > C.hierclass: Designing classes in a hierarchy: > <a name="rh-vbase"></a>C.137: Use `virtual` bases to avoid overly general base classes > Reason > Example

##### Example

 struct Interface {
 virtual void f();
 virtual int g();
 // ... no data here ...
 };

 class Utility { // with data
 void utility1();
 virtual void utility2(); // customization point
 public:
 int x;
 int y;
 };

 class Derive1 : public Interface, virtual protected Utility {
 // override Interface functions
 // Maybe override Utility virtual functions
 // ...
 };

 class Derive2 : public Interface, virtual protected Utility {
 // override Interface functions
 // Maybe override Utility virtual functions
 // ...
 };

Factoring out `Utility` makes sense if many derived classes share significant "implementation details."

