Source: Cpp Core Guidelines > <a name="s-expr"></a>ES: Expressions and statements > <a name="res-lib"></a>ES.1: Prefer the standard library to other libraries and to "handcrafted code" > <a name="res-abstr"></a>ES.2: Prefer suitable abstractions to direct use of language features > Reason > Example

##### Example

 vector<string> read1(istream& is) // good
 {
 vector<string> res;
 for (string s; is >> s;)
 res.push_back(s);
 return res;
 }

The more traditional and lower-level near-equivalent is longer, messier, harder to get right, and most likely slower:

 char** read2(istream& is, int maxelem, int maxstring, int* nread) // bad: verbose and incomplete
 {
 auto res = new char*[maxelem];
 int elemcount = 0;
 while (is && elemcount < maxelem) {
 auto s = new char[maxstring];
 is.read(s, maxstring);
 res[elemcount++] = s;
 }
 *nread = elemcount;
 return res;
 }

Once the checking for overflow and error handling has been added that code gets quite messy, and there is the problem remembering to `delete` the returned pointer and the C-style strings that array contains.

