Source: Cpp Core Guidelines > <a name="s-expr"></a>ES: Expressions and statements > ES.dcl: Declarations > <a name="res-reuse"></a>ES.12: Do not reuse names in nested scopes > Reason > Exception

##### Exception

We often reuse function names from a base class in a derived class:

 struct B {
 void f(int);
 };

 struct D : B {
 void f(double);
 using B::f;
 };

This is error-prone.
For example, had we forgotten the using declaration, a call `d.f(1)` would not have found the `int` version of `f`.

??? Do we need a specific rule about shadowing/hiding in class hierarchies?

