Source: Cpp Core Guidelines > <a name="s-expr"></a>ES: Expressions and statements > ES.dcl: Declarations > <a name="res-unique"></a>ES.24: Use a `unique_ptr<T>` to hold pointers > Reason > Example

##### Example

 void use(bool leak)
 {
 auto p1 = make_unique<int>(7); // OK
 int* p2 = new int{7}; // bad: might leak
 // ... no assignment to p2 ...
 if (leak) return;
 // ... no assignment to p2 ...
 vector<int> v(7);
 v.at(7) = 0; // exception thrown
 delete p2; // too late to prevent leaks
 // ...
 }

If `leak == true` the object pointed to by `p2` is leaked and the object pointed to by `p1` is not.
The same is the case when `at()` throws. In both cases, the `delete p2` statement is not reached.

