Source: Cpp Core Guidelines > <a name="s-expr"></a>ES: Expressions and statements > ES.expr: Expressions > <a name="res-casts"></a>ES.48: Avoid casts > Reason > Example, bad

##### Example, bad

 double d = 2;
 auto p = (long*)&d;
 auto q = (long long*)&d;
 cout << d << ' ' << *p << ' ' << *q << '\n';

What would you think this fragment prints? The result is at best implementation defined. I got

 2 0 4611686018427387904

Adding

 *q = 666;
 cout << d << ' ' << *p << ' ' << *q << '\n';

I got

 3.29048e-321 666 666

Surprised? It is actually undefined behavior, and so could also have crashed the program.

