Source: Cpp Core Guidelines > <a name="s-expr"></a>ES: Expressions and statements > <a name="ss-numbers"></a>Arithmetic > <a name="res-signed"></a>ES.102: Use signed types for arithmetic > Reason > Example

##### Example

The standard library uses unsigned types for subscripts.
The built-in array uses signed types for subscripts.
This makes surprises (and bugs) inevitable.

 int a[10];
 for (int i = 0; i < 10; ++i) a[i] = i;
 vector<int> v(10);
 // compares signed to unsigned; some compilers warn, but we should not
 for (gsl::index i = 0; i < v.size(); ++i) v[i] = i;

 int a2[-2]; // error: negative size

 // OK, but the number of ints (4294967294) is so large that we should get an exception
 vector<int> v2(-2);

 Use `gsl::index` for subscripts; [see ES.107](#res-subscripts).

