Source: Cpp Core Guidelines > <a name="s-concurrency"></a>CP: Concurrency and parallelism > <a name="rconc-multi"></a>CP.1: Assume that your code will run as part of a multi-threaded program > Reason > Example, bad

##### Example, bad

 double cached_computation(int x)
 {
 // bad: these statics cause data races in multi-threaded usage
 static int cached_x = 0.0;
 static double cached_result = COMPUTATION_OF_ZERO;

 if (cached_x != x) {
 cached_x = x;
 cached_result = computation(x);
 }
 return cached_result;
 }

Although `cached_computation` works perfectly in a single-threaded environment, in a multi-threaded environment the two `static` variables result in data races and thus undefined behavior.

