Source: Cpp Core Guidelines > <a name="s-concurrency"></a>CP: Concurrency and parallelism > <a name="sscp-con"></a>CP.con: Concurrency > <a name="rconc-lock"></a>CP.21: Use `std::lock()` or `std::scoped_lock` to acquire multiple `mutex`es > Reason > Example

##### Example

This is asking for deadlock:

 // thread 1
 lock_guard<mutex> lck1(m1);
 lock_guard<mutex> lck2(m2);

 // thread 2
 lock_guard<mutex> lck2(m2);
 lock_guard<mutex> lck1(m1);

Instead, use `lock()`:

 // thread 1
 lock(m1, m2);
 lock_guard<mutex> lck1(m1, adopt_lock);
 lock_guard<mutex> lck2(m2, adopt_lock);

 // thread 2
 lock(m2, m1);
 lock_guard<mutex> lck2(m2, adopt_lock);
 lock_guard<mutex> lck1(m1, adopt_lock);

or (better, but C++17 only):

 // thread 1
 scoped_lock<mutex, mutex> lck1(m1, m2);

 // thread 2
 scoped_lock<mutex, mutex> lck2(m2, m1);

Here, the writers of `thread1` and `thread2` are still not agreeing on the order of the `mutex`es, but order no longer matters.

