Source: Cpp Core Guidelines > <a name="s-concurrency"></a>CP: Concurrency and parallelism > <a name="sscp-con"></a>CP.con: Concurrency > <a name="rconc-wait"></a>CP.42: Don't `wait` without a condition > Reason > Example, bad

##### Example, bad

 std::condition_variable cv;
 std::mutex mx;

 void thread1()
 {
 while (true) {
 // do some work ...
 std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(mx);
 cv.notify_one(); // wake other thread
 }
 }

 void thread2()
 {
 while (true) {
 std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lock(mx);
 cv.wait(lock); // might block forever
 // do work ...
 }
 }

Here, if some other `thread` consumes `thread1`'s notification, `thread2` can wait forever.

