Source: Cpp Core Guidelines > <a name="s-errors"></a>E: Error handling > <a name="re-design"></a>E.1: Develop an error-handling strategy early in a design > <a name="re-errors"></a>E.3: Use exceptions for error handling only > Reason > Example, don't

##### Example, don't

 // don't: exception not used for error handling
 int find_index(vector<string>& vec, const string& x)
 {
 try {
 for (gsl::index i = 0; i < vec.size(); ++i)
 if (vec[i] == x) throw i; // found x
 }
 catch (int i) {
 return i;
 }
 return -1; // not found
 }

This is more complicated and most likely runs much slower than the obvious alternative.
There is nothing exceptional about finding a value in a `vector`.

