Source: Cpp Core Guidelines > <a name="s-errors"></a>E: Error handling > <a name="re-design"></a>E.1: Develop an error-handling strategy early in a design > <a name="re-catch"></a>E.18: Minimize the use of explicit `try`/`catch` > Reason > Example, Bad

##### Example, Bad

 void f(zstring s)
 {
 Gadget* p;
 try {
 p = new Gadget(s);
 // ...
 delete p;
 }
 catch (Gadget_construction_failure) {
 delete p;
 throw;
 }
 }

This code is messy.
There could be a leak from the naked pointer in the `try` block.
Not all exceptions are handled.
`deleting` an object that failed to construct is almost certainly a mistake.
Better:

 void f2(zstring s)
 {
 Gadget g {s};
 }

