Source: Cpp Core Guidelines > <a name="s-errors"></a>E: Error handling > <a name="re-design"></a>E.1: Develop an error-handling strategy early in a design > <a name="re-no-throw-crash"></a>E.26: If you can't throw exceptions, consider failing fast > Reason > Example

##### Example

 void f(int n)
 {
 // ...
 p = static_cast<X*>(malloc(n * sizeof(X)));
 if (!p) abort(); // abort if memory is exhausted
 // ...
 }

Most programs cannot handle memory exhaustion gracefully anyway. This is roughly equivalent to

 void f(int n)
 {
 // ...
 p = new X[n]; // throw if memory is exhausted (by default, terminate)
 // ...
 }

Typically, it is a good idea to log the reason for the "crash" before exiting.

