Source: Cpp Core Guidelines > <a name="s-stdlib"></a>SL: The Standard Library > <a name="ss-con"></a>SL.con: Containers > <a name="rsl-bounds"></a>SL.con.3: Avoid bounds errors > Reason > Example, bad

##### Example, bad

 void f()
 {
 array<int, 10> a, b;
 memset(a.data(), 0, 10); // BAD, and contains a length error (length = 10 * sizeof(int))
 memcmp(a.data(), b.data(), 10); // BAD, and contains a length error (length = 10 * sizeof(int))
 }

Also, `std::array<>::fill()` or `std::fill()` or even an empty initializer are better candidates than `memset()`.

