Source: Cpp Core Guidelines > <a name="s-stdlib"></a>SL: The Standard Library > <a name="ss-con"></a>SL.con: Containers > <a name="rsl-copy"></a>SL.con.4: don't use `memset` or `memcpy` for arguments that are not trivially-copyable > Reason > Example

##### Example

 struct base {
 virtual void update() = 0;
 };

 struct derived : public base {
 void update() override {}
 };

 void f(derived& a, derived& b) // goodbye v-tables
 {
 memset(&a, 0, sizeof(derived));
 memcpy(&a, &b, sizeof(derived));
 memcmp(&a, &b, sizeof(derived));
 }

Instead, define proper default initialization, copy, and comparison functions

 void g(derived& a, derived& b)
 {
 a = {}; // default initialize
 b = a; // copy
 if (a == b) do_something(a, b);
 }

