Source: Clang Tidy Checks > cppcoreguidelines-prefer-member-initializer > Example 1

## Example 1

```c++
class C {
 int n;
 int m;
public:
 C() {
 n = 1; // Literal in default constructor
 if (dice())
 return;
 m = 1;
 }
};
```

Here `n` can be initialized in the constructor initializer list, unlike
`m`, as `m`'s initialization follows a control statement (`if`):

```c++
class C {
 int n;
 int m;
public:
 C(): n(1) {
 if (dice())
 return;
 m = 1;
 }
};
```

